WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.900
JOE: It is not always obvious when
an individual with autism is going

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.420
to behave in a challenging manner,
but there are ways to help prevent

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:11.329
this happening and if it does happen
there are also ways to de-escalate the

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.520
situation or cope with the behaviour.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.429
BETH: The first thing a person should do
in all situations is to remain calm so

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:20.480
that they can in turn calm the behaviour.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:25.530
Being calm, gentle and patient and
giving an individual with autism space

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:27.669
can sometimes be all that is needed.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.860
JOE: If an individual show signs of
behaviour that challenges it can be

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:36.580
good to find alternative activities or
objects to focus on to relieve the sensory

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.460
stimuli that may be affecting them.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.940
BETH: Providing alternative objects
to play with, that provide a

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:45.919
similar sensory experience to that
provided by the behaviour, can help

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.589
to distract them and this can be
incorporated into their daily routine.

00:00:50.009 --> 00:00:55.489
For a person who bites, this could be
to provide chewy tubes, gum, carrots,

00:00:55.489 --> 00:01:03.839
raw pasta or sultanas, or for a person
pinches, they could be given playdough.

00:01:03.839 --> 00:01:08.722
JOE: Opportunities for relaxation could
be created wherever possible, such

00:01:08.722 --> 00:01:15.210
as watching bubble lamps, smelling
essential oils, as long as they are not

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:19.980
oversensitive to smells, listening to
music, or using a swing or rocking chair.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.710
Distressed behaviour can be diffused by
an activity that releases energy like

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:28.910
punching a punch bag, bouncing on a
trampoline or running around the garden.

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:33.429
BETH: Reducing stressors by removing
distracting elements, going to a

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:37.850
less stressful place or providing a
calming activity or object, can help

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:40.220
to focus on returning to a calm state.

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:43.800
JOE: When supporting somebody who
is stressed, it is essential to

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.620
keep calm and quiet and to be a
consistent, safe presence to help

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.610
them feel they can begin to relax.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:54.429
If this is not the case it may make
them feel less secure and more anxious.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.880
Visual supports can be used to help
with de-escalating a situation.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.159
BETH: It can also be helpful to
discuss the situation with the

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:06.399
individual once calm has been achieved
and try to teach them alternate

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:08.000
and more appropriate responses.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.829
This should be done providing clear
directions and using simple language and

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:16.950
attempts by the individual with autism
to self-regulate should be praised.

